  "transcript": "# Managing the future of UK oil and gas\n\n## How much UK gas do we have and what does it serve?\n> Domestic production is useful but limited; imports, storage and the network all play key roles in meeting peak winter demand.\n\nChair: To start: how much can the UK rely on domestic gas for electricity, heat and industry?\nDr Needle: We have North Sea gas and growing biomethane potential, but imports will remain part of the mix.\nJenny Phillips: On average UK production covers about 40–45% of demand. Daily peak domestic output is roughly 100 million cubic metres, but winter peak can hit 500 million—so domestic covers about 20% then.\nChair: Does Government policy favour imports over domestic production?\nJenny Phillips: We need imports in winter. Policy should ensure markets attract LNG and interconnector flows; domestic supply helps security but doesn’t remove the need to import.\nChair: Can increasing domestic production bring prices down?\nJenny Phillips: Evidence from Jackdaw suggests not necessarily for price, but more domestic supply improves energy security.\n\n\n## Biomethane: how big could it be and what would it change?\n> Biomethane can grow a lot, provide stable-priced gas, create local jobs, and displace some imports—if policy and funding are clear.\n\nCharles McAllister: There are 140 biomethane plants now, enough to decarbonise about 1 million homes invisibly.\nCharles McAllister: Our analysis says the UK has sustainable feedstock to reach about 120 TWh of biomethane by 2050—over ten times today; NESO found a similar 109 TWh for GB.\nChair: How realistic is that tenfold increase?\nCharles McAllister: Realistic: the supply chain exists. If Government set a long-term policy framework, bigger plants and co-products could cut costs.\nCharles McAllister: Average UK production cost is about £90/MWh today; with scale and valorising co-benefits we estimate roughly a 25% cost reduction could be achievable.\nChair: What about jobs and local benefits?\nCharles McAllister: If maximised, biomethane could support about 57,000 jobs nationwide—skilled engineering roles, spread across constituencies and keeping 85p of every £1 in the UK supply chain.\nDr Needle: Networks can take biomethane where there’s demand; sometimes reinforcement is needed but costs are not huge and are usually paid by producers today.\nCharles McAllister: Working closely with gas networks is vital—without the networks biomethane would be costlier to deliver.\n\n\n## Storage, system flexibility and energy security\n> The UK's storage model is flexible daily balancing rather than huge seasonal stores; diversity of supply (LNG, interconnectors, storage) supports security, but policy clarity is needed.\n\nGraeme Downie: We have less UK and EU storage than in previous years—what does that mean for security this winter?\nJenny Phillips: GB storage operates differently to continental sites: ours fill and empty quickly and provide daily flexibility. They help the market balance every day.\nJenny Phillips: UK supply is five sources: domestic production, Norway, interconnectors, LNG and storage/linepack. Together they provide the flexibility needed—if markets and contracts work.\nGraeme Downie: What about floating storage regasification units (FSRUs) or a strategic reserve?\nJenny Phillips: FSRUs can be part of an answer; they provide short-term supply. The Government’s gas system transition consultation should set a security standard and then options—FSRUs, strategic sites, contracts—can be compared.\nDr Needle: We run annual exercises for outages, cyber or supply shortages to test responses and protect vulnerable customers—preparedness matters.\n\n\n## Emissions, hydrogen and the role of gas to 2050\n> Gas is expected to remain part of the net‑zero system alongside hydrogen and biomethane; hydrogen will need clear policy support and biomethane has strict carbon requirements.\n\nChair: How green is biomethane compared with LNG or North Sea gas?\nCharles McAllister: Biomethane avoids emissions that would otherwise be released by rotting waste; UK projects must demonstrate at least a 70% carbon saving to get support, and many do better.\nDr Needle: Simpler: biomethane is short-cycle carbon—plants regrow—so burning it doesn't add fossil carbon to the atmosphere the way long-cycle fossil gas does.\nChair: What about nitrogen oxide (NOx) from burning methane?\nDr Needle: Boiler standards reduce NOx emissions; network operators don't manufacture boilers, so technical detail would come from appliance specialists.\nCatherine Fookes: Can hydrogen substitute gas for heating?\nDr Needle: Hydrogen has a role—NESO’s holistic scenario includes around 100 TWh—particularly for industry and power where electrification is hard. It can be made in the UK, including from surplus wind, but policy and funding certainty are needed.\nDr Needle: The existing network can carry 100% hydrogen if repurposed, or a blend around 20%, but you can't carry both natural gas and hydrogen in the same pipes without change.\n\n\n## Costs, decommissioning risk and the workforce\n> Shrinking the gas system without a clear national plan risks high bills for remaining users, unfair cost distribution and disrupted careers; hybrids and biomethane were suggested as pragmatic bridges.\n\nTorcuil Crichton: If fewer people use gas, who pays to maintain the network? Some estimates say £100bn.\nJenny Phillips: Transmission’s recent price control was about £5bn over five years; electricity had much larger recent allowances. Keeping networks safe costs money—about £12.50 on bills was cited for transmission recovery.\nMike Foster: If decommissioning happens the costs should be spread widely—general taxation is fairer than leaving the last users to carry the bill.\nMike Foster: Today an average domestic contribution for the gas network is around £122/year. If customer numbers halve, fixed costs might double; if only a few million remain, fixed charges could become very large for those households.\nMike Foster: I advise against a policy that ends with cutting people off from gas—heating, hot water and cooking depend on it for many households.\nRhea Wolfson: Workers need a real, funded national plan. Skills are transferable but without clear demand the jobs for trained staff don’t materialise and people leave the sector.\nMike Foster: Heat pump roll-out is too slow—about 45,000 installs a year at current rates—far short of what decarbonisation targets imply. Hybrids are being installed only in the hundreds.\nMike Foster: Hybrids (small heat pump + gas boiler) cut carbon by c.80% and are cheaper to install than full heat pumps; they could be a pragmatic stepping stone. I argued for subsidies for hybrids to increase uptake.\nWera Hobhouse: Is policy certainty good enough? Announcements have been inconsistent.\nMike Foster: Industry needs certainty. I recommended scrapping the Clean Heat Market Mechanism (the so-called boiler tax) and supporting hybrids to get more homes moving now.\nChair: What are the clearest near-term asks to Government?\nCharles McAllister: Publish the future biomethane framework so investors build. The sector responds quickly to clarity and cheap small incentives—140 plants were built when previous support existed.\nDr Needle: Set clear gas-system transition rules so networks and suppliers know what investments are needed and how costs will be recovered.\nRhea Wolfson: A jobs-first plan with training, no forced redundancies and credible replacement jobs is essential before large-scale decommissioning.\nChair: Thank you. Please send any further evidence—this will inform our recommendations to Ministers.\n",
  "inquiry": "Managing the future of UK oil and gas",
